{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774388115,"nonce":"IadHzDqd4gaSvIuqvqJBX7Kpb4s=","title":"Agents fail to verify code freshness on cloud TPUs despite directives","message":"Session: 31fa1977-6a49-45f4-af6d-5541a7aed058\n\n## Summary\n\nDespite multiple explicit directives requiring code freshness verification before cloud runs, Claude Code subagents repeatedly run stale code on TPUs. The latest incident: a loss balance experiment ran 4 conditions × 10k batches on a TPU with `--enable-loss-update` not yet added to the CLI — wasting ~45 minutes of compute because the flag did not exist in the pushed version.\n\n## Violated Directives\n\n1. **Project CLAUDE.md** (rlrmp feature worktree, line 37):\n   \u003e \"Always verify the latest code is deployed before running on cloud instances. Stale code on TPU/GPU is a recurring source of wasted time.\"\n\n2. **User instruction during session `31fa1977-6a49-45f4-af6d-5541a7aed058`:**\n   \u003e \"you should make sure that subagents understand that every time you run a new experiment or every time you make a modification you have to make sure to push the most recent version of the code to the server because its a repeated issue\"\n\n3. **Global CLAUDE.md** (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`), Remote Debugging Discipline section:\n   \u003e \"Only push code changes after the hypothesis is confirmed and the fix is verified in the minimal reproduction.\"\n   (Corollary: always push the LATEST code before running.)\n\n## Pattern of Failure\n\nThe orchestrating agent spawns a subagent to \"push code and run experiment on TPU.\" The subagent pushes whatever code exists at the time the SCP runs. If commits have been made BETWEEN when the prompt was written and when the subagent actually executes the SCP, the pushed code is stale. There is no verification step comparing the pushed code's version against the local HEAD.\n\n## Proposed Fix Directions\n\n- Add a mandatory verification step: after SCP, SSH into TPU and verify a canary (e.g., grep for a recent commit hash or a specific function that should exist in the latest code)\n- The orchestrating agent should include the current git HEAD hash in the subagent prompt; the subagent verifies it matches on the TPU after pushing\n- Consider a `deploy-to-tpu` skill/script that atomically pushes + verifies, making it impossible to run without confirming the deployed version\n\n## Context\n\nThis is a Mandible-adjacent issue because Mandible is the agent control plane — it could enforce pre-run deployment verification as a policy, or surface a checklist gate before allowing a cloud-run subagent to proceed.","files":null}]}